  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 339 PM CDT Wed May 20 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nShallow upper troughing aloft will influence our region's\nweather through Saturday. Shortwave trough within this flow will\nbring widespread rain Friday and Saturday. Prior to this\noccurring, presently dry air mass over the Northern Plains and\nUpper Midwest combine with gusty winds and lingering dry fuels\ncontributing to near critical to critical fire weather\nconditions this afternoon and Thursday.\n\nInto next week, flow becomes more zonal to southwesterly amid\nupper ridging into the Northern Plains by mid to late next week.\nThis will increase overall temperature trend, allowing more\nsummer-like temperatures into the 80s across our area by\nTuesday, perhaps as early as Monday. This increases the chance\nfor instability and some moisture to work its way into our\nregion, however flow aloft is favored to be on the lighter side,\nlimiting the potential for organization of thunderstorms \n(should they occur), as well as limited chance for more robust\nforcing aloft to spark convection. There is still a chance for \ndisorganized scattered showers and storms as ignited by other \nsources of forcing like surface/topographically induced \nforcing mid to late next week, with confidence in details like\nlocation and timing very low.\n\n...Fire weather conditions...\n\nGusty south to southeast winds between lee surface troughing in\nthe western Dakotas and a departing surface high pressure will\nkeep winds elevated today, tonight, and Thursday. The pressure\ngradient and mixing winds aloft are not overly strong, generally\naround 15-30 mph. Gusts will be more intermittent tonight\ncompared to this afternoon and Thursday. Gusts are expected to\nramp up quickly in the morning as daytime mixes into the\nresidual overnight low level jet.\n\nThursday's RH values are forecast to be in the 30s percent, \nwith upper 20s near central MN. There is a 20% chance RH values \nin the upper 20s are more widespread through the rest of MN into\nRed River Valley.\n\nOverlap of highest winds and lowest RH values currently looks \nto be within the Red River Valley, with around a 20% chance to\nsee critical fire weather conditions here late morning through\nearly afternoon.",
